WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:02.760
So you know these are city planners who built to last.

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:08.660
I mean downtown Turin has about a megaton
of these surviving 400 year old baroque buildings

00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:10.120
They are very hard to destroy.

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:14.160
They're mostly brick.
Because brick absorbs cannon fire.

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:16.260
But there's some marble.

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:21.520
Even by military standards, these are some
alarmingly stout urban structures.

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:24.940
Some of them get abandoned for 100 years or more.

00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:28.260
They don't fall down. They don't rot. They don't collapse.

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:32.440
They just silently endure they're like Egyptian pyramids.

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:34.500
And eventually people show up again.

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:36.340
And they tend to just loft them out.

00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:41.640
So now they're all over the place.
They're not aristocratic buildings, of course.

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:47.600
They're museums, archives, hospitals, cultural centers, grocery stores, tourist attractions...

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:52.379
Futuristic planned palaces that just won't go away.

00:00:52.379 --> 00:00:56.140
They just don't wear out that's why their futuristic.

00:00:56.140 --> 00:00:58.020
It has been there all the time.

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:00.980
And I have one of my own now.

00:01:00.980 --> 00:01:12.700
I've got some postcards of my pet Turinese
375-year-old palace. Oh yeah, thank you, Stewart.

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:18.360
Yes, that's our latest Turinese renewal project.
"The Phoenix Renewed".

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:25.000
We're trying to find a new tenant who will move into
and renovate this palace.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:32.500
This tenant needs to stay there for about 60 years. They're trying to find a guy with some staying power.

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:39.040
It'll take about $150K to maintain the palace
and it's gardens which are very extensive.

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:47.580
It's like four stories high. But it basically has about the same square cubic meterage as your odd building here.

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:52.060
You're in a stuffed animal building of some kind.
And this is a similar thing.

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:58.780
It's got the volume of your thing, but it's just...
it's baroque and it's 17th century.

00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:04.500
And it's got like paintings and statuary
and chandeliers and carpets.

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:06.800
And that's our writer office.

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:13.340
We were done with our house of the future project
in our factory.

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:16.560
When we got this call from the local cultural foundation

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:20.600
who are called the Company of Saint Paul:
the Compagnia di San Paolo.

00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:23.800
They're a non-governmental enterprise in Turin. An NGO.

00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:25.580
And hey're 500 years old.

00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:28.940
They've been around half a millennium.

00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:32.580
So these guys are running this 500 year old charity

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:37.260
and they happen to be fans of ours.
It turns out they really like to read science fiction.

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:44.380
So they asked us if we would leave our house of the future and do a video to advertise one of their palaces.

00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:46.660
To attract possible new tenants.

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:50.800
Because they manage this brick palace
which is up in the hills.

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:54.100
And it's called the
"Delightful Vineyard of the Royal Madame"

00:02:54.100 --> 00:02:59.960
("the vineyard Madama Reale"). It's a place
with a rather checkered history.

00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:04.080
It turns out that this palace/villa, our new office,

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:09.810
was built by and for a guy who was one of
my favorite Turinese historical characters.

00:03:09.810 --> 00:03:14.380
I didn't realize it was actually his palace.
Until we'd like moved into the place.

00:03:14.380 --> 00:03:18.537
But I consider him a kind of ultimate
Turinese historical character.

00:03:18.537 --> 00:03:21.700
I know a lot about him.
He's a super interesting guy to me.

00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:25.537
I just never expected
that he would become my landlord.

00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:31.380
So we went into this baroque palace and they gave us the formal tour of the entire thing top to bottom.

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:36.180
We went up the attic which is this
amazing looking crooked garret.

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:39.460
We said, wow, what a fantastic office
for creative writers.

00:03:39.460 --> 00:03:43.540
And actually the entire building was a historical archive

00:03:43.540 --> 00:03:51.400
that was its most recent use. Mostly it was stuffed full of 500 years of financial records. Centuries of them.

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:53.900
Just all over the place. Financial banking records.

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:58.980
But it was empty because the archivist had
digitized the records and they'd left the place.

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:04.420
So it was record free, and they
were looking for a new tenant.

00:04:04.420 --> 00:04:11.320
So we volunteered. We moved in. We don't reside
in our palace. We certainly can't afford it.

00:04:11.320 --> 00:04:15.500
There are no bedrooms in there. But when we're in Turin

00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:18.320
we commute to our palace at 9am.

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:23.260
And we go up to the attic. We plug in the old computers. And we have at some creative writing

00:04:23.260 --> 00:04:26.020
in the palace and then we leave promptly at 5pm

00:04:26.020 --> 00:04:28.440
because they lock the whole place up electronically.

00:04:28.442 --> 00:04:33.120
And we go back to our actual apartment which
is in a completely different area of Turin.

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:36.600
We don't rent the palace. They don't pay us to be there.

00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:40.340
Just kind of a friendly understanding.
We're just residents in the palace.

00:04:40.340 --> 00:04:44.820
I'm the "Visionary in Residence" at the Villa Abegg.

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:48.120
And you know we write novels about Turin.

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:52.840
And except for one security guard,
bored guy downstairs reading sci-fi novels,

00:04:52.840 --> 00:04:56.440
we are the only people in this huge ancient building.

00:04:56.440 --> 00:05:00.460
We're just like the modern ghosts
in the 375 year old structure.

00:05:00.460 --> 00:05:04.620
It's just us and that statuary and the priceless paintings and the chandeliers...

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:10.080
and the giant carpets and the many other relics
and the huge weedy garden and the secret tunnels...

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:13.540
and the weird lake with the opera set on a little island...

00:05:13.540 --> 00:05:17.660
and a greenhouse and big urns
that are shrouded in ivy...

00:05:17.660 --> 00:05:22.080
And it's not a house of the future,
but it is our palace of the past.

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:24.720
When we're not around we really kind of miss it.

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:28.380
It's surprising how quickly you can
get used to palatial surroundings.

00:05:28.380 --> 00:05:36.820
I know this sounds rather comic: the sci-fi writer
is marooned inside the ancient palace.

00:05:36.820 --> 00:05:40.540
It's like an Italo Calvino story, right?

00:05:40.540 --> 00:05:45.620
It's oxymoronic. It's got a lot of trick reversal
kind of stuff going on.

00:05:45.620 --> 00:05:49.380
But it is certainly the weirdest thing
that ever happened to me in Turin.

00:05:49.380 --> 00:05:55.360
And it has some element of karmic retribution.

00:05:55.360 --> 00:06:01.940
It's like, you're a guy, a futurist,
and you claim to be outside of time.

00:06:01.940 --> 00:06:06.060
You have some some higher understanding
of historic processes.

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:10.760
And you even have the power to do weird things
in empty factories.

00:06:10.760 --> 00:06:13.060
Because you can convince people to help you.

00:06:13.060 --> 00:06:17.640
But that car Factory is merely 100 years old.

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:26.060
You should try some of your cheap futurist magic tricks with this other building from another pace layer entirely.

00:06:26.060 --> 00:06:30.100
375 years old!

00:06:30.100 --> 00:06:32.300
UNESCO World Heritage Age.

